                  %div
                    %p
                      %a{:href => "/nlab/edit/equality+and+equivalence+-+contents"} Edit this sidebar
          %h1#weak_homotopy_equivalences Weak homotopy equivalences
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition_for_topological_spaces_and_simplicial_sets"} Definition (for topological spaces and simplicial sets)
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#EquivalentCharacterizations"} Equivalent characterizations
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#RelationToHomotopyEquivalences"} Relation to homotopy equivalences
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#RelationToHomotopyTypes"} Relation to homotopy types
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#RelationToFreeHomotopySets"} Relation to free homotopy sets
              %li
                %a{:href => "#for_other_kinds_of_spaces"} For other kinds of spaces
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#ExamplesOfNonReversibleWHEs"} Of non-reversible weak homotopy equivalences
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
            is a map between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            or similar which induces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
            on all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            is called a
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            of the categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            at the weak homotopy equivalences used as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            yields the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28Top%29"} Ho(Top)
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28sSet%29"} Ho(sSet)
            or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
          %p
            Weak homotopy equivalences are named after
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"} homotopy equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domains"} domains
            by a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphisms"} quasi-isomorphisms
            and
            = succeed "-equivalences." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homotopy"} chain homotopy
          %p
            From another perspective, the notion of
            %em weak homotopy equivalence
            is ‘observational’, in that a map is a weak homotopy equivalence if when we look at it through the observations that we can make of it using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
            or even the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+infinity-groupoid"} fundamental infinity-groupoid
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
            is more ‘constructive’; in that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is a homotopy equivalence if there exists an inverse for it (up to homotopy, of course). Note that both of these notions are weaker than mere
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            of topological spaces (homeomorphism) and so can be considered examples of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalence"} weak equivalence
          %p There are actually two related concepts here: whether two spaces are weakly homotopy equivalent and whether a map between spaces is a weak homotopy equivalence. The former is usually defined in terms of the latter.
          %h2#definition_for_topological_spaces_and_simplicial_sets Definition (for topological spaces and simplicial sets)
          #WeakHomotopyEquivalence.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              or
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              two
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              or
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
              or
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              between them is called a
              %strong weak homotopy equivalence
              if
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  induces an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                  of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
                  (path components in the case of topological spaces)
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi Π
                          %mn 0
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\Pi_0(f) \colon \Pi_0(X) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \Pi_0(Y)
                %p
                  in
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              %li
                %p
                  for all
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/points"} points
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in X
                  and for all
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi ℕ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 \leq n) \in \mathbb{N}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  induces an isomorphism on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups"} homotopy groups
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mover
                          %mo →
                          %mo ≃
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Y
                        %mo ,
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\pi_n(f,x) \colon \pi_n(X,x) \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \pi_n(Y,f(x))
                %p
                  in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grp"} Grp
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+space"} path-connected
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              in each path
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+component"} connected component
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              It is not enough to require that each pair of homotopy groups are isomorphic. For example, let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∨
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y = S^1 \vee S^2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be its double cover, which glues two spheres at the north and south poles of a circle. Then there are isomorphisms on the fundamental group
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≃
                      %mi ℤ
                      %mo ≃
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X) \simeq \mathbb{Z} \simeq \pi_1(Y)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_1(X) \to \pi_1(Y)
              induced by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is not an isomorphism. And by the properties of covering spaces, all higher homotopy groups are isomorphic. But these two are not weakly homotopy equivalent.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              There are many alternative definitions of weak homotopy equivalences. A simplicial map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a weak equivalence of simplicial sets if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ex
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex^\infty f
              is a simplicial homotopy equivalence if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(f,A)
              is a simplicial homotopy equivalence for any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              has a right relative-homotopy-lifting property with respect to the maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∂
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial\Delta^n\to\Delta^n
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a composition of an acyclic cofibration (i.e., a map with a left lifting property with respect to all maps with a right lifting property with respect to horn inclusions) and an acyclic fibration (i.e., a map with a right lifting property with respect to inclusions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \partial\Delta^n\to\Delta^n)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a weak equivalence of topological spaces if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi Sing
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |Sing(f)|
              is a homotopy equivalence of topological spaces if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hom(A,f)
              is a homotopy equivalence for any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW-complex"} CW-complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              has a right relative-homotopy-lifting property with respect to the maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1}\to D^n
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is a composition of an acyclic Serre cofibration (a retract of a relative CW-complex) and an acyclic
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibration"} Serre fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |-|
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sing
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sing
              preserve and reflect weak equivalences, so any of the two classes defines the other.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              with respect to weak homotopy equivalences is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28Top%29"} Ho(Top)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                        %mi whe
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_{whe}
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              Accordingly, weak homotopy equivalences are the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
              in the standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} Quillen model structure on topological spaces
              and the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} Quillen model structure on simplicial sets
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mixed+model+structure"} mixed model structure
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#EquivalentCharacterizations Equivalent characterizations
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              A continuous map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
              is a weak homotopy equivalence precisely if for all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in \mathbb{N}
              and for all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagrams"} commuting diagrams
              of continuous maps of the form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo −
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    S^{n-1} &amp;\to&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                    \\\
                    D^n &amp;\to&amp; Y
                    }
                    \\,,
            %p
              where the left morphism is the inclusion of the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n-1)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
              as the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
              of the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ball"} ball
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : D^n \to X
              that makes the resulting upper triangle commute and such that the lower triangle commutes up to a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi S
                              %mrow
                                %mi n
                                %mo −
                                %mn 1
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⇙
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi D
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    S^{n-1} &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; X
                    \\\
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \nearrow &amp; \swArrow&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                    \\\\\
                    D^n &amp;&amp;\to&amp;&amp; Y
                    }
            %p
              which is constant along
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo ↪
                      %msup
                        %mi D
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1} \hookrightarrow D^n
          %p
            In this form the statement and its proof appears in (
            %a{:href => "#Jardine"}> Jardine
            ) (where it is also generalized to weak equivalences in a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Lurie"} Lurie, prop. 6.5.2.1
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#May"} May, section 9.6
            %em HELP lemma
            in (
            %a{:href => "#Vogt"}> Vogt
            ).
          %h3#RelationToHomotopyEquivalences Relation to homotopy equivalences
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Every
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              is a weak homotopy equivalence.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              It requires a little bit of thought to prove this, because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              and its homotopy inverse
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              need not preserve any chosen basepoint. But for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X
              and any
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\ge 1
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↗
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ↘
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ,
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ⟶
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi π
                              %mi n
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ,
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi x
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \\pi_n(X,x) &amp; &amp; \to &amp; &amp; \pi_n(X,g(f(x)))
                    \\\
                    \&amp;
                    \\searrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\nearrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\searrow
                    \\\
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\pi_n(Y,f(x))
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \&amp;&amp;
                    \\pi_n\big(
                    Y,f(g(f(x)))
                    \\big)
                    }
            %p
              in which the two horizontal morphisms are
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphisms"} isomorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g f
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f g
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopic
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+morphism"} identities
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/two-out-of-six+property"} two-out-of-six property
              for isomorphisms, the diagonal morphisms are also all isomorphisms.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              Conversely, any weak homotopy equivalence between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/m-cofibrant+spaces"} m-cofibrant spaces
              (spaces that are homotopy equivalent to
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CW+complexes"} CW complexes
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
          %h3#RelationToHomotopyTypes Relation to homotopy types
          %p
            We discuss the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
            generated by weak homotopy equivalence, called
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                (weak)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "quasi-isomorphism#RelationToChainHomologyType"} Relation to homology type
          #ReflexiveAndTransitiveButNotSymmetric.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              The existence of a weak homotopy equivalence from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflexive+relation"} reflexive
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transitive+relation"} transitive relation
              on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+relation"} symmetric relation
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Reflexivity and transitivity are trivially checked. A counterexample to symmetry is example
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CircleAndPseudoCircle"}
              below.
          %p
            But we can consider the genuine equivalence relation
            %em generated
            by weak homotopy equivalence:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              We say two spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              have the same
              %strong
                (weak)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
              if they are equivalent under the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+relation"} equivalence relation
              %em generated
              by weak homotopy equivalence.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              Equivalently this means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              have the same (weak) homotopy type if there exists a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zigzag"} zigzag
              of weak homotopy equivalences
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ←
                    %mo →
                    %mo ←
                    %mi …
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \leftarrow \to\leftarrow \dots \to Y
                    \\,.
            %p
              This in turn is equivalent to saying that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              become
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
              = succeed "/" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28Top%29"} Ho(Top)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28sSet%29"} Ho(sSet)
              with the weak homotopy equivalences
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} inverted
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Two spaces
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
              may have isomorphic homotopy groups without being weak homotopy equivalence: for this all the isomorphisms must be induced by an actual map
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo :
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            %p
              However, if, roughly, one remembers, how all the homotopy groups
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} act
              on each other, then this is enough information to exhibit the full homotopy type. This collection of data is called the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Postnikov+tower"} Postnikov tower
              decomposition of a homotopy type.
          %h3#RelationToFreeHomotopySets Relation to free homotopy sets
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo ,
                    %mi X
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi TopSp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K, X \,\in\, TopSp
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(K, X)
            for their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basepoint"} basepoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Map(S^1, Y)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+loop+space"} free loop space
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/based+loop+space"} based loop space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi x
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi fib
                        %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi ev
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Map
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ↠
                    %mrow
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi ev
                        %mi x
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Omega_x X
                  \\xhookrightarrow{\; fib_x(ev_\ast) \;}
                  Map(S^1, X)
                  \\overset{\; ev_x \;}{\twoheadrightarrow}
                  X
          %p
            for any base point
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \,\in\, X
